    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#VM">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character"/><orig><unclear>Ι</unclear>ΟΣ</orig> <space extent="unknown" unit="character"/> <orig><unclear>Ι</unclear>Α<unclear>Ι</unclear></orig> <space extent="unknown" unit="character"/> <gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character"/><orig><unclear>ΛΣΙΛ</unclear></orig>
                    <lb n="2"/><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character"/><placeName type="ethnic" ref="http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/462086"><supplied reason="lost">Ἀκρ</supplied><unclear>α</unclear>γαντῖνος</placeName>.
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text after Arena 2002 (IGASMG II² 93).</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="line 1">
                        <note>Daux 1937 tentatively interpreted the surviving traces as the end of a personal name in <foreign xml:lang="grc">-ιος</foreign> or <foreign xml:lang="grc">-ος</foreign>, followed perhaps by the patronymic <foreign xml:lang="grc">Μνασία</foreign>.</note>
                    </app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p>[— — —], an Akragantine.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>The ethnic Ἀκραγαντῖνος is the only securely interpretable element. Daux regarded the text as a dedication rather than an artist's signature and attributed the form of gamma to an Akragantine stonecutter. 
                    Bousquet interpreted the holes on the upper bed as settings for at least two bronze statues and at least two stelae, suggesting an elongated base carrying a multi-figure dedication.</p>
            </div>
